WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.297
Prompt injection defense is the discipline of building AI applications so untrusted natural-language content cannot quietly take control of the system.

2
00:00:10.297 --> 00:00:20.436
In a Python application, prompts may come from users, documents, tickets, web pages, messages, code repositories, tool outputs, or retrieval systems.

3
00:00:20.436 --> 00:00:27.903
Some of that content can look like instructions, but the application must decide what authority it actually has.

4
00:00:27.903 --> 00:00:32.069
Defense begins with treating language as input, not as policy.

5
00:00:32.069 --> 00:00:40.873
Prompt injection is different from an ordinary user instruction, a model error, a hallucination, or a pure access-control failure.

6
00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:44.410
A normal user instruction expresses the user's goal.

7
00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:48.654
A hallucination is an incorrect or unsupported model response.

8
00:00:48.654 --> 00:00:56.278
An access-control failure occurs when the application exposes or modifies something without proper permission.

9
00:00:56.278 --> 00:01:08.855
Prompt injection is the risk that untrusted content changes model behavior in a way the application did not intend, especially when the output affects data, tools, decisions, or workflows.

10
00:01:08.855 --> 00:01:15.300
Direct prompt injection comes from content the user intentionally enters into the AI interface.

11
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:29.998
Indirect prompt injection comes from content the system later reads, retrieves, summarizes, or processes, such as documents, emails, web pages, PDFs, support tickets, code comments, logs, or knowledge-base articles.

12
00:01:29.998 --> 00:01:42.810
The defensive pattern is the same at a high level: understand where content came from, what authority it has, what it is allowed to influence, and what controls must be enforced outside the model.

13
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:47.919
Prompt injection defense is application security, not only prompt writing.

14
00:01:47.919 --> 00:01:58.766
Clear prompts help guide behavior, but they do not reliably enforce authorization, tenant boundaries, data access, tool permissions, or high-impact approvals.

15
00:01:58.766 --> 00:02:08.591
Python code, identity systems, retrieval filters, tool wrappers, output validators, logs, tests, and monitoring all participate in the defense.

16
00:02:08.591 --> 00:02:15.194
The model can assist the workflow, but the application must remain in charge of security decisions.
